    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Determining the moral justification for preemptive military action is deeply complex, often polarizing political and ethical views.\n\nA strong argument favoring it might lean towards a **Realist or Utilitarian framework,** suggesting that the \"greatest good for the greatest number\" is served by stopping the potential large-scale harm of an authoritarian expansion before it begins.\n\nHowever, this is heavily contested by **Deontological ethics,** which might argue that the act of starting war, regardless of the outcome, is inherently a violation of fundamental moral and legal duties, like the right to self-defense against an attack that has not yet occurred.\n\nThere is no single definitive moral framework that universally supports this, as it demands a high burden of proof to justify the massive moral and political risk of war.",
